The frame dump writes the vertex shader constants, once per frame.

The dump names the shaders and the textures but not the numbers they are
fed, and several things we need are frame constants the engine uploads —
the fog above all. BF2 ships its shaders as source (`Shaders_client.zip`),
so what `FogRange` is *used* for is readable (`RaCommon.fx:54`), but what
it *is* is packed by the engine, and the name is nowhere in `BF2.exe` —
neither as a string nor as a semantic, so the binding lives in the
compiled effect and the code addresses parameters by handle. A frame-dump
measurement is a source under rule 6; a guess is not.

One line per non-zero constant row of **both** stages, on every fortieth
draw of a dumped frame. Per frame would be enough for a frame constant,
but the first draw of a frame turned out to be a shadow or interface pass
whose constants hold no fog at all — a handful of snapshots across the
frame catches the passes that do, without burying an 850-call log.

The pixel stage is there because the terrain's sun and sky arrive as pixel
constants and the vertex ones cannot answer for them:
`psc c0 = 0.1875 0.1775 0.1425` on Karkand is `terrain.sunColor` quartered.

`OPENBF2_DUMP_CONSTANTS` changes the period — a number, or `all` for every
draw, or `off`. Every draw is what a question about one surface needs
(which colours a leaf is given, say), and a frame of 600 draws then costs
some twelve thousand lines: right for one press, ruinous as a default.
`tools/bf2_run.sh` passes it as `BF2_CONSTANTS`.

To read the result, take a dump on two levels whose fog differs —
`Renderer.fogStartEndAndBase` is 0/135 on Strike at Karkand and 0/610 on
Dalian Plant — and the row that moves with them is the one.

Applies on top of `tools/mtld3d_frame_dump_geom.patch`, to
`reference/mtld3d`, before `tools/mtld3d_build.sh`.

--- a/windows/d3d9/src/device/frame_dump.rs
+++ b/windows/d3d9/src/device/frame_dump.rs
@@ -357,5 +480,71 @@ impl DeviceInner {
                 c[78]
             );
         }
+
+        // [openbf2] The frame's vertex constants.
+        //
+        // Everything the engine uploads per frame rather than per draw lands
+        // here, and none of it is in the game's data files. The fog is the
+        // reason this exists: BF2 ships its shaders as source, so what
+        // `FogRange` is *used* for is readable (`RaCommon.fx:54`), but what it
+        // *is* is packed by the engine and its name is nowhere in `BF2.exe` —
+        // neither as a string nor as a semantic.
+        //
+        // Every fortieth draw by default, not just the first. A frame constant
+        // would need only one snapshot, but the first draw of a frame turned
+        // out to be a shadow or interface pass whose constants hold no fog at
+        // all; a handful across the frame catches the passes that do without
+        // burying an 850-call log. Not at frame start either — there the effect
+        // has not set its parameters yet.
+        //
+        // `OPENBF2_DUMP_CONSTANTS` changes the period: a number, or `all` for
+        // every draw. Every draw is what a question about one surface needs —
+        // which colours a leaf is given, say — and a frame of 600 draws then
+        // costs some twelve thousand lines, which is fine for one press and
+        // ruinous as a default.
+        //
+        // Both stages: the terrain's sun and sky reach the shader as pixel
+        // constants, and the vertex ones alone cannot answer for them.
+        let period = openbf2_constant_period();
+        if period != 0 && seq % period == 0 {
+            let vs = self.shader_bindings().vs_constants_copy();
+            for (row, value) in vs.iter().enumerate() {
+                if value.iter().all(|component| *component == 0.0) {
+                    continue;
+                }
+                info!(
+                    target: LOG_TARGET,
+                    "[dump] draw {seq} vsc c{row}: {:.6} {:.6} {:.6} {:.6}",
+                    value[0], value[1], value[2], value[3]
+                );
+            }
+            let ps = self.shader_bindings().ps_constants_copy();
+            for (row, value) in ps.iter().enumerate() {
+                if value.iter().all(|component| *component == 0.0) {
+                    continue;
+                }
+                info!(
+                    target: LOG_TARGET,
+                    "[dump] draw {seq} psc c{row}: {:.6} {:.6} {:.6} {:.6}",
+                    value[0], value[1], value[2], value[3]
+                );
+            }
+        }
     }
 }
+
+/// [openbf2] How often the dump writes a draw's shader constants: the value of
+/// `OPENBF2_DUMP_CONSTANTS`, which is a period, or `all` for every draw, or
+/// `off`. Read once — the variable is set before the game starts.
+fn openbf2_constant_period() -> u32 {
+    use std::sync::OnceLock;
+    static PERIOD: OnceLock<u32> = OnceLock::new();
+    *PERIOD.get_or_init(|| match std::env::var("OPENBF2_DUMP_CONSTANTS") {
+        Ok(text) => match text.trim().to_ascii_lowercase().as_str() {
+            "all" | "every" | "1" => 1,
+            "off" | "none" | "0" => 0,
+            other => other.parse::<u32>().unwrap_or(40),
+        },
+        Err(_) => 40,
+    })
+}
